State file holds the important information, such as resource ID of the resources that it created, metadata 
of the resources IP addresses, if exists for the resources. It also holds the information of dependencies between the resources.
And finally, it holds the information of last known state of the infrastructure.

Now, because this information is not only important, but it is also very sensitive for the organization, for example IP addresses of the instances.
So this cannot be stored on the local disk of the individual DevOps engineer.
At the same time, this cannot be stored on a git repository as well. Because if this state file. With all this information is stored in a git repository.
If the git repository is public. Everyone can access it. And your compromise with the sensitive information. Even if the git repository is private within the organization.
Still a lot of people can access this information. So to avoid this state, file is stored in remote backends like AWS, S3 or Azure Blob.

All that you need to do is you need to define a backend.tf. Within your CL files and within this backend.tf, you provide the information of the remote backend
that you're going to use. Just create the remote backend such as create S3 bucket on AWS. And within the backend tf.
Just provide the name, region and other information so that Terraform can upload the state file onto these remote backends.

So within the remote backend you can enable versioning. So it provides the capabilities like git where the remote backends can also act as version control systems.
You can enable security so that your state file is secure. You can block access with strict Rbac and policies. You can also enable backups.
And finally locking of the state file within the remote backend. Now locking also provides a great advantage because Terraform files cannot be executed by 
multiple DevOps engineers concurrently.  
```
Let's say you have a Terraform file to create EC2 instance.
You cannot have two DevOps engineers run the same EC2 instance file with the same username, because Terraform will confuse which instruction to pick.
Whereas if one developer executes the Terraform file, EC2 instance is created. 
Now, if the second engineer tries to use the same HCL file, AWS will return an error saying that EC2 instance exists or any relevant error.
It doesn't have to be with creation of instance only. It can be with updating same resource by multiple DevOps engineers.

Even in this case, let's say two DevOps engineers tries to update an existing resource on AWS. Terraform will fail to understand which instruction it has to take.
Locking the state file will provide the flexibility. So once this engineer executes, the instruction state file is logged on the particular instance name.
Only once the execution is complete, lock is released. Then other DevOps engineer gets access to the log file.
So this is how you explain why you store a terraform state file in the git repository instead of S3 backend or any other remote backend.
